WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.373
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book two.

2
00:00:05.673 --> 00:00:08.967
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:16.795
Unit seventeen: Reader-Response Theory. Today you will make careful, hedged claims about meaning with may, might and could.

4
00:00:17.095 --> 00:00:19.690
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:23.843
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:26.010
Reader-response.

7
00:00:27.017 --> 00:00:31.524
Reader-response means a theory focusing on the reader's role in creating meaning.

8
00:00:32.117 --> 00:00:35.509
Reader-response criticism values personal reactions.

9
00:00:36.117 --> 00:00:38.711
Your turn. Say: Reader-response.

10
00:00:41.567 --> 00:00:42.788
Reader-response.

11
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:44.818
Interpretation.

12
00:00:45.817 --> 00:00:49.465
Interpretation means the meaning a reader constructs from a text.

13
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.735
Two valid interpretations can coexist.

14
00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:56.478
Your turn. Say: Interpretation.

15
00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:00.648
Interpretation.

16
00:01:01.383 --> 00:01:02.663
Subjectivity.

17
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:07.844
Subjectivity means the influence of personal feelings and experience.

18
00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.995
Subjectivity shapes what we notice in a poem.

19
00:01:11.583 --> 00:01:14.619
Your turn. Say: Subjectivity.

20
00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:18.665
Subjectivity.

21
00:01:19.417 --> 00:01:21.335
Horizon of expectation.

22
00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:27.008
Horizon of expectation means the assumptions readers bring from their time and culture.

23
00:01:27.617 --> 00:01:30.975
Modern readers have a different horizon of expectation.

24
00:01:31.583 --> 00:01:35.095
Your turn. Say: Horizon of expectation.

25
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:39.840
Horizon of expectation.

26
00:01:40.583 --> 00:01:42.293
Meaning-making.

27
00:01:43.283 --> 00:01:46.804
Meaning-making means the active process of constructing meaning.

28
00:01:47.417 --> 00:01:49.950
Reading is a form of meaning-making.

29
00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.353
Your turn. Say: Meaning-making.

30
00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.991
Meaning-making.

31
00:01:58.750 --> 00:01:59.693
Reception.

32
00:02:00.683 --> 00:02:04.076
Reception means how a work is received by readers over time.

33
00:02:04.683 --> 00:02:07.252
The novel's reception changed after the war.

34
00:02:07.850 --> 00:02:10.363
Your turn. Say: Reception.

35
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.248
Reception.

36
00:02:14.983 --> 00:02:15.996
Engagement.

37
00:02:16.983 --> 00:02:19.935
Engagement means active involvement with a text.

38
00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:22.817
Annotation increases engagement.

39
00:02:23.417 --> 00:02:25.790
Your turn. Say: Engagement.

40
00:02:28.633 --> 00:02:29.704
Engagement.

41
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.439
Co-creation.

42
00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.994
Co-creation means the joint production of meaning by author and reader.

43
00:02:36.583 --> 00:02:39.279
Meaning is a co-creation between text and reader.

44
00:02:39.883 --> 00:02:42.838
Your turn. Say: Co-creation.

45
00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:46.748
Co-creation.

46
00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:51.509
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:59.658
Visal, two students read the same poem about a river: one found it hopeful, the other deeply sad. Who is right?

48
00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:09.010
Possibly both. A text's meaning might shift depending on the reader, especially when the poem itself is ambiguous.

49
00:03:10.117 --> 00:03:12.906
Doesn't that mean any interpretation is acceptable?

50
00:03:14.017 --> 00:03:23.252
Not quite. A reading could be personal, but it should still be supported by evidence in the text, or it may become pure invention.

51
00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:26.477
Why might their readings differ so much?

52
00:03:27.583 --> 00:03:36.447
Their horizons of expectation may differ. A student who has lost someone could read the river as loss, rather than renewal.

53
00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:39.309
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:43.369
Grammar focus: Hedged claims (may, might, could).

55
00:03:43.669 --> 00:03:44.913
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.617 --> 00:03:48.348
A text's meaning might shift depending on the reader.

57
00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:52.515
Their horizons of expectation may differ significantly.

58
00:03:53.417 --> 00:03:56.879
A personal reading could reveal something the author never intended.

59
00:03:57.833 --> 00:04:08.415
Academic writers hedge to show caution: may / might / could, often with 'arguably', 'to some extent' or 'it seems that'. Hedging is not weakness; it signals that a claim is open to discussion.

60
00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:12.720
Be careful! Don't say: A text's meaning might shifts.

61
00:04:14.667 --> 00:04:17.282
Say: A text's meaning might shift.

62
00:04:17.582 --> 00:04:19.767
After 'might', use the base verb.

63
00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:23.289
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:25.275
The Reader's Role.

65
00:04:25.575 --> 00:04:30.663
For much of the twentieth century, critics treated the meaning of a text as fixed within its words.

66
00:04:31.667 --> 00:04:37.637
Reader-response theorists challenged this view, arguing that meaning may only come into being through the act of reading.

67
00:04:38.633 --> 00:04:45.428
Each reader brings a horizon of expectation, which might include cultural assumptions, memories and earlier reading.

68
00:04:46.433 --> 00:04:52.972
This does not mean that all interpretations are equally valid; a reading could still be judged by its use of evidence.

69
00:04:53.967 --> 00:04:59.263
Instead, meaning may best be described as a co-creation between the text and its readers.

70
00:05:00.367 --> 00:05:04.368
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:05.217 --> 00:05:07.286
Choose the best hedged claim.

72
00:05:07.586 --> 00:05:16.290
A, This poem is definitely about death.. B, This poem may be read as a meditation on loss.. C, This poem is about death, everyone agrees..

73
00:05:19.833 --> 00:05:24.329
The answer is B: This poem may be read as a meditation on loss..

74
00:05:25.483 --> 00:05:28.597
Their readings blank differ because of their experiences.

75
00:05:28.897 --> 00:05:32.574
A, might. B, might to. C, might be to.

76
00:05:36.133 --> 00:05:37.936
The answer is A: might.

77
00:05:39.083 --> 00:05:42.255
The assumptions readers bring from their culture form their blank.

78
00:05:42.555 --> 00:05:47.683
A, horizon of expectation. B, reception. C, co-creation.

79
00:05:51.233 --> 00:05:54.057
The answer is A: horizon of expectation.

80
00:05:55.217 --> 00:05:57.263
Which phrase is a hedging device?

81
00:05:57.563 --> 00:06:01.495
A, clearly. B, it seems that. C, without doubt.

82
00:06:05.033 --> 00:06:07.428
The answer is B: it seems that.

83
00:06:08.583 --> 00:06:12.348
According to the reading, how can interpretations still be judged?

84
00:06:12.648 --> 00:06:17.752
A, by their length. B, by their use of evidence. C, by the author's approval.

85
00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:24.089
The answer is B: by their use of evidence.

86
00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:28.347
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:29.183 --> 00:06:32.890
Do you think a text has one fixed meaning, or many depending on the reader?

88
00:06:49.683 --> 00:06:51.207
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:51.507 --> 00:07:21.021
I think a text may have several valid meanings, although not an unlimited number. Every reader brings different experiences, so the same story might feel comforting to one person and painful to another. For example, a novel about migration could be read as an adventure by some and as a story of loss by others. Critics who defend a single meaning argue that the author's intention matters. I agree it matters, but I believe readers complete the text.

90
00:07:21.321 --> 00:07:23.007
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:23.917 --> 00:07:28.392
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and hedged claims (may, might, could).

92
00:07:28.692 --> 00:07:33.643
Today's value is open-mindedness. Listen to new ideas before you judge them.

93
00:07:33.943 --> 00:07:37.071
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:37.371 --> 00:07:40.314
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

